WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.440
A cockpit display has to stay readable in direct sun at 85

2
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.200
degrees and a picture that quietly stops updating is a hazard.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.080
SKU-8 checks every frame in hardware.

4
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.325
Video arrives over LVDS, MIPI or parallel RGB,

5
00:00:16.325 --> 00:00:20.885
and lands in a small line buffer, so the link never has

6
00:00:20.885 --> 00:00:22.595
to keep the panel's pace.

7
00:00:22.595 --> 00:00:25.585
Liquid crystal changes its response as it heats.

8
00:00:25.585 --> 00:00:29.655
So every pixel is corrected against a temperature sensor on the chip,

9
00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:32.195
and contrast holds in the sun.

10
00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:35.089
Every column of the panel has its own converter.

11
00:00:35.629 --> 00:00:41.449
Here, the fine digital work ends and the high-voltage drive begins.

12
00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:46.787
High-voltage amplifiers swing 3,840 outputs across 12 volts,

13
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:54.067
voltages the finest chips cannot reach, and sharing charge between columns saves

14
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.167
power.

15
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.591
A timing controller paces every frame and scans the rows at up

16
00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:02.951
to 24 volts, while the backlight dims in eight local zones.

17
00:01:03.191 --> 00:01:06.151
Hardware compares each frame with the one before.

18
00:01:06.151 --> 00:01:11.831
If the picture stops changing, the operator is warned within two frames.

19
00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:14.038
SKU-8 is pre-silicon.

20
00:01:14.038 --> 00:01:16.938
No frame has been driven on silicon yet,

21
00:01:16.938 --> 00:01:19.318
and every figure is a target

22
00:01:20.398 --> 00:01:22.498
Bring us your panel and your environment.

23
00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:26.158
We will tell you what SKU-8 must prove.
